2026-02-03 12:00:01 [scudmarks@discord] are the input tokens charged similar to the context windows size tokens quoted in models?
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] No. You are charged for input tokens consumed (what you actually send), not the context
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] window capacity.
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] Key differences:
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] • Context window (e.g., 128K, 200K) is the maximum the model can hold. You pay for
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] usage within that limit.
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] • Input tokens charged (`usage.prompt_tokens` in the API response) is the actual count of
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] your prompt + system message + conversation history + any file shares.
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] From your `slopfountain.ts` relay logic:
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] const spent=[usage.prompt_tokens | 0,usage.completion_tokens | 0];
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] // ...
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] spend=spent[0]*tokenRate/1e6+spent[1]*outputRate/1e6
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] You pay for `spent[0]` (input) and `spent[1]` (output). If your conversation grows large, input
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] costs rise because you're sending the full history each turn until you `/reset` or `/drop` shares.
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] • Exception: Some providers (Anthropic, DeepSeek) offer prompt caching* where
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] cached input tokens are cheaper than fresh ones—shown in your code where `rate.length>2` includes a
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] `cachedRate` at index 1.
2026-02-03 12:00:21 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:01:05 [scudmarks@discord] can i calculate % of context window used?
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] Yes. You have both values available in the code:
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] • Before sending* (rough estimate):
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] const info = modelSpecs[grokModel];
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] const maxTokens = info?.maxprompt || 0;
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] const currentBytes = measure(rohaHistory); // your existing function
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] const estimatedTokens = currentBytes / 4;  // rough heuristic: ~4 bytes/token
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] const percentUsed = (estimatedTokens / maxTokens * 100).toFixed(1);
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] echo(`Context: ${percentUsed}% of ${maxTokens}K window`);
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] • After sending* (exact):
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] const usage = completion.usage;
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] const promptTokens = usage.prompt_tokens;
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] const maxTokens = modelSpecs[grokModel]?.maxprompt || 1;
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] const percentUsed = (promptTokens / maxTokens * 100).toFixed(1);
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] echo(`Used ${percentUsed}% of context (${promptTokens}/${maxTokens})`);
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] • Caveat*: Your `measure()` function returns bytes (from `JSON.stringify`), while context
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] windows are quoted in tokens. The 4:1 ratio is approximate—for exact pre-flight checks you'd need
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] to run the tokenizer, which most providers don't expose client-side. The post-call percentage using
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] `usage.prompt_tokens` is precise.
2026-02-03 12:01:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
