2026-02-04 00:17:46 [scudmarks@discord] what produces the sound when laser cleaner sweeps across surface?
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] When a laser cleaner sweeps across a surface, the sound primarily comes from rapid thermal
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] expansion and explosive vaporization of contaminants and surface material.
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] Key mechanisms producing the acoustic signature:
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] • Thermal shock and ablation* – The high-energy laser pulses (typically nanosecond or
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] femtosecond duration) instantly heat absorbed contaminants to thousands of degrees Celsius, causing
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] them to vaporize or sublimate. This creates miniature explosions at the surface.
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] • Laser-induced plasma* – At sufficient energy densities, the vaporized material ionizes,
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] forming a rapidly expanding plasma plume. This expansion creates shock waves in the surrounding
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] air, producing the characteristic popping or crackling sound.
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] • Mechanical vibration* – The thermal stress induces rapid expansion and contraction in
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] both the contaminant layer and substrate, generating audible vibrations.
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] The specific acoustic signature varies with laser parameters: pulse duration, wavelength, fluence
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] (energy density), and the composition of what's being removed (rust, paint, oxides, or biological
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] material). Higher fluence produces louder, sharper reports as more material undergoes phase change
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] and plasma formation.
2026-02-04 00:18:12 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:32:00 [scudmarks@discord] the sound is very similar to splash screen of williams arcade machines, any ideas of the algorithms / effects at play in their 6800 code?
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] The sonic signature you're hearing—the crackling, swept "white noise whoosh"—comes from
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] software-synthesized stochastic audio on the Williams 6800 hardware. Both the laser cleaner
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] and the Williams splash screens produce band-limited noise with amplitude modulation and
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] frequency sweep characteristics, though via entirely different physical mechanisms.
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] Williams 6800 Sound Architecture
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] Williams machines (Defender, Robotron, Joust, Stargate) used a dedicated Motorola 6800 CPU
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] driving an 8-bit DAC directly—no AY-3-8910, no SID, no POKEY. The 6800 spent ~90% of its cycles
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] in tight interrupt service routines (ISRs) calculating waveform samples and blasting them to the
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] DAC at ~8-10kHz.
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] The "splash screen" algorithm (often called the "swoosh" or "wipe" effect) typically combined three
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] elements:
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 1. LFSR White Noise Generator
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] The stochastic crackle came from a Linear Feedback Shift Register (LFSR)—usually 16-bit or
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 24-bit with taps at bits 16, 15, 1, 0 (XORed together). This produced pseudo-random values that
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] sound like acoustic white noise when streamed to the DAC.
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] ; 6800 pseudocode - LFSR noise generator
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] NOISE:  LSR NOISE_HI    ; Shift right
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]         ROR NOISE_LO    ; Rotate through carry
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]         BCC NO_OUT      ; If carry clear, output
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]         LDA NOISE_LO    ; Get random value
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]         EOR #$FF        ; Invert (optional for balance)
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]         STAA DAC        ; Output to DAC
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] NO_OUT: RTS
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 2. Frequency Sweep via Decimation
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] To create the "sweeping" quality (like the laser moving across the screen), the code decimated
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] the LFSR output—updating the DAC only every N cycles, where N increased
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] exponentially or linearly over time. This effectively lowers the cutoff frequency of the noise,
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] creating the characteristic downward pitch sweep from "hiss" to "thump."
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] // Conceptual C for the sweep logic
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] uint16_t lfsr = 0xACE1;
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] uint8_t decimation = 1;      // Start fast (high frequency)
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] uint8_t decay = 255;         // Amplitude envelope
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] while(decay > 0) {
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]     if(--decimation == 0) {
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]         // Tap LFSR
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]         lfsr = (lfsr >> 1) ^ (-(lfsr & 1u) & 0xB400);
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]         // Output with amplitude envelope
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]         DAC = ((lfsr & 0xFF) * decay) >> 8;
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]         decimation = sweep_rate++;  // Slow down update = lower pitch
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]         decay -= envelope_step;     // Fade out
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5]     }
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 3. Amplitude Envelope
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] An exponential decay table (stored in ROM) multiplied the raw noise values, creating the "doppler
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] fade" as the "splash" completes.
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] Why They Sound Identical
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] ╭───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] │  Laser Cleaner                                                                    │  Williams 6800 Code                                        │
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] ├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] │  **Plasma micro-explosions** create random acoustic impulses                      │  **LFSR bit transitions** create random digital impulses   │
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] │  **Sweep velocity** modulates the rate of popping (Doppler-like)                  │  **Decimation rate** modulates the noise cutoff frequency  │
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] │  **Surface irregularities** cause amplitude variation (some spots ablate louder)  │  **Envelope generator** causes amplitude decay             │
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] │  **Broadband "crackle"** from stochastic vaporization                             │  **Broadband "crackle"** from pseudo-random bitstream      │
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] ╰───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] Both are impulse-driven broadband noise sources with swept low-pass filtering—the laser
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] acoustically filters itself as it moves (directionality and distance effects), while the 6800
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] mathematically filtered the LFSR by skipping samples.
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] Specific Williams Implementation
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] Eugene Jarvis and Larry DeMar often used a "swept noise table" rather than real-time LFSR
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] for the splash effect in later games (Robotron onward). They pre-calculated 256-512 samples of
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] white noise with an integrated envelope, then played it back with a variable phase
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] increment—essentially wavetable synthesis where the playback rate slowed down over time,
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] stretching the noise and lowering its apparent pitch simultaneously.
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] This creates that distinctive "electric waterfall" sound that fades into sub-bass
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] rumble—mathematically identical to the sound of a laser cleaner sweeping across a rusted surface
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] where the pop density decreases as the beam moves away or the debris clears.
2026-02-04 00:33:10 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:36:02 [scudmarks@discord] nice, and all that with your context window fill of my awful typescript
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] The TypeScript architecture is actually quite sophisticated—modular provider adapters, clean
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] separation between transport layers (SSH/WebSocket/Unix sockets), and that raw-mode terminal
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] handler with grapheme clustering is solid work.
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] The context window is fine; the codebase tells a clear story of incremental refinement from "roha"
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] → "foundry" → "forge" → "fountain." The LFSR noise analogy holds up because both systems are
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] essentially stochastic impulse generators with time-varying spectral density—whether the
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] randomness comes from plasma physics or XOR taps at bits 16/15/1/0.
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] If you ever want to synthesize that laser sound digitally, a 24-bit Galois LFSR with polynomial
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] `0x80000057` run through a variable-rate decimator and exponential envelope would get you 95% of
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] the way there. No requests for code dumps here.
2026-02-04 00:36:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:39:22 [scudmarks@discord] would tools for reverse engineering (machine code -> assembler -> high level description) be ethical for vintage 6800 williams coinops?
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] For vintage 6800 Williams coin-ops (Defender, Robotron, Joust, Stargate—circa 1980–1984), the
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] ethical landscape favors preservation and archival research over strict copyright
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] adherence, provided the work is non-commercial and educational.
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] • Context matters:*
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] - Cultural heritage: These represent foundational digital artifacts. The 6800 assembly is a
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] dead language commercially; no revenue stream exists for the original ROMs as arcade software.
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] - Abandonment: Williams Electronics (now under Scientific Games/ Light & Wonder) hasn't
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] monetized these specific 6800 implementations in decades. The hardware is unobtainable except via
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] restoration.
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] - Creator intent: Eugene Jarvis and Larry DeMar have historically supported the
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] preservation community, recognizing that emulation and reverse engineering keep these mechanical
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] artworks alive.
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] • Ethical boundaries:*
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] - ✓ Archival: Decoding 6800 opcodes to understand collision detection algorithms or sound
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] synthesis (your LFSR discussion) serves historical documentation.
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] - ✓ Educational: Translating assembly to C pseudocode for academic study of real-time 6800
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] interrupt handling.
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] - ✗ Commercial: Selling reprogrammed ROMs or cloned hardware without licensing.
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] - ✗ Circumvention: Breaking modern encryption on current Williams/Bally slot machines
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] (active revenue products).
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] • The "gray": Technically, these ROMs remain under copyright until ~2075–2095 (corporate
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] authorship, 95-year term). However, ethical use aligns with fair use* principles:
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] transformative (analysis vs. consumption), non-commercial, and no market harm (the market for 1981
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] Defender ROMs is zero).
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] If your tooling produces high-level descriptions of audio algorithms or collision physics rather
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] than drop-in replacement code, it functions as digital archaeology—ethical and culturally
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] valuable.
2026-02-04 00:39:46 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:40:54 [scudmarks@discord] original roms still feature on modern console retro collections - so point 1 - nah
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] You are correct. If the ROMs are still featured in current commercial retro collections (Midway
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] Arcade Origins, Arcade Archives, Atari 50, etc.), that invalidates the "abandonment" argument
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] entirely. Active monetization changes the ethical calculus.
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] • Corrected analysis:*
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] If Defender or Robotron are currently generating revenue on Switch/PlayStation/Xbox stores, then
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] machine-code-to-C decompilation tools targeting those specific ROMs ethically require:
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] 1. Legitimate ownership: You must own the specific ROM image through purchase (arcade
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] board, licensed compilation, etc.)
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] 2. No redistribution: The derived high-level descriptions or reconstructed source must
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] remain private research, not published
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] 3. Non-commercial use: Creating a "Defender clone" from reverse-engineered 6800 code
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] competes directly with current sales
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] 4. Clean-room separation: Understanding the algorithms (permissible) vs. copying the
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] specific expression (copyright infringement)
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] • The distinction that survives:*
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] - Studying generic 6800 sound synthesis techniques (the LFSR/decimator architecture we
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] discussed) is ethical—those are methods, not expression
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] - Automated tools that process specific copyrighted ROM bytes into readable high-level code
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] strays into infringement when the output reveals the protected creative expression (level layouts,
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] timing constants, specific audio tables)
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] If the goal is educational understanding of Williams-era audio engineering, working from
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] documented techniques (Jarvis interviews, contemporary trade publications) rather than
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] disassembled ROMs maintains ethical integrity while the titles remain commercially active.
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] Your point stands: currently sold retro games deserve the same IP respect as modern
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] releases, regardless of their age or processor architecture.
2026-02-04 00:41:15 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:21 [scudmarks@discord] how about mods that enhance original roms, is that a thing?
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] Yes, ROM hacking/enhancement patches for Williams 6800 coin-ops exist, though the scene is
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] smaller than console hacking (NES/SNES) due to the hardware barrier—you need the actual PCB or
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] accurate emulation to test.
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] The Technical Reality
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 6800 ROM mods are feasible because:
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] - Flat memory maps: Early Williams games (Defender, Stargate) use straightforward 16KB–48KB
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] ROM layouts without encryption
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] - Assembly-level patches: Hacks modify specific subroutines—e.g., changing `$A1B4` from
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] `DEC LIVES` to `NOP NOP` for infinite lives, or tweaking the LFSR seed values we discussed to alter
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] sound timbres
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] - Checksum bypass: Many boards have ROM checksum verification; mods must patch the checksum
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] routine or recalculate the expected values
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] Common Enhancement Types
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] • Bug fixes*: Defender has a notorious ship-spawn bug where you can crash immediately on
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] new life. Community patches fix the initialization timing.
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] • Difficulty balancing*: "Turbo" variants increase enemy counts or speed beyond the
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 255-maximum limitations of the original counters (requires changing `CMP #$FF` to higher values
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] with bank switching).
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] • Sound modifications*: Hacks that replace the LFSR polynomials or envelope
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] rates—essentially the sonic "mods" you were theorizing about.
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] • Multi-region*: Converting "Red Label" Defender to "Green Label" behavior (official ROM
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] revisions treated as "mods" by the community).
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] The Legal Trap You Identified
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] Since these ROMs remain commercially active (Midway Arcade Origins, etc.), distributing
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] complete modified ROM images is straightforward copyright infringement—no different than
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] distributing a patched Mario ROM.
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] The patch model (IPS/UPS/BPS files) exists in a deliberate gray area:
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] - The patch contains only the differences (XOR deltas)
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] - User must supply the original copyrighted ROM
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] - Legal theory: No copyrighted bytes distributed; only instructions to transform
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] - Reality: Still likely contributory infringement, but unenforced for abandoned titles (which these
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] are not, as you noted)
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] The Williams-Specific Wrinkle
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] Williams' later 6800 games (Sinistar, Bubbles) used ROM banking and watchdog timers
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] that make naive mods crash the hardware. The enhancement scene requires understanding the specific
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] memory-mapped I/O (C000–CFFF range) and the dual-6800 synchronization in Robotron/Stargate.
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] • Bottom line*: Technically trivial for a 6800 assembly programmer; legally problematic
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] given current commercial availability; ethically contested between "archival preservation" and
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] "active IP violation."
2026-02-04 00:43:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:21 [scudmarks@discord] what is state of atari vector game machines, is it possible to build a vector display with modern components without resorting to raster based emulation?
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Original Atari vector hardware (Asteroids, Tempest, Star Wars, Battlezone) survives in collector
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] hands but faces a catastrophic parts shortage: the specialized Wells-Gardner and Amplifone
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] XY monitors are irreplaceable, and the custom vector generator ASICs (POKEY variants, AVG/IVG
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] chips) are increasingly scarce.
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] True Vector Without Raster Emulation
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] • Yes, absolutely possible*, but requires abandoning the CRT-specific deflection approach
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] in favor of modern vector projection or display technologies:
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 1. Laser Galvanometer Systems
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] The most viable modern vector display. Two-axis galvanometer mirrors steer a laser beam with
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] microsecond precision—essentially the same principle as Atari's electron beam steering, but with
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] photons instead of electrons.
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] - Commercial: Pangolin FB3/FB4 controllers accept vector data via ILDA protocol
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] - DIY: Teensy 4.1 + DAC (MCP4922) driving Chinese galvos (~$200) can render Asteroids at
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 30k points/second
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] - Trade-off: No phosphor persistence, so "trail" effects require deliberate frame buffering
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] (unlike CRT afterglow)
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 2. Oscilloscope Vector Conversion
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] X-Y mode on modern digital scopes (Rigol, Siglent) accepts analog voltages to drive the display
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] directly. Vector game PCBs output X/Y/Z (brightness) analog signals naturally—just need level
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] shifting to ±5V or ±10V scope inputs.
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] - Limitation: Small screen, non-square aspect ratio, burn-in risk with persistence modes
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] - Advantage: True analog deflection, no pixels, perfect mathematical purity
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 3. Vector Monitors from Test Equipment
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Surplus storage tube displays (Tektronix 4010 series) or analog radar scopes provide genuine
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] large-format vector display, though refresh rates are poor compared to gaming requirements.
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 4. Custom Electromagnetic Deflection
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Building a CRT-based XY monitor from scratch is theoretically possible but practically insane: you
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] need custom-wound yokes, high-voltage flyback transformers, and 50MHz+ deflection amplifiers. A few
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] hobbyists have done it; none have mass-produced it.
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] The Modern Bottleneck
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Atari's vector hardware generated analog X/Y/Z signals at 1-4 MHz update rates. Modern
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] solutions must replicate that analog bandwidth:
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Original Atari AVG (Analog Vector Generator)
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5]     ↓
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Digital Vector Data (6502/vector state machine)
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5]     ↓
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] DAC (10-bit, ~2MHz update)
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5]     ↓
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Deflection Amplifier (±70V, 10MHz bandwidth)
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5]     ↓
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] CRT Yoke (magnetic deflection)
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] • Modern equivalent:*
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Teensy 4.1 or FPGA
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5]     ↓
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Dual DAC (MCP4822, SPI, 12-bit)
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5]     ↓
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Galvo driver amplifier (±10V, analog servo loop)
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5]     ↓
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Galvanometer mirrors (600°/s slew rate)
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] The "MAME Problem"
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Standard MAME vector emulation rasterizes lines into pixel buffers. This destroys:
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] - Line thickness modulation (varying Z-axis intensity changes line width on real vectors;
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] raster just changes alpha)
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] - Phosphor bloom (the CRT glow physics)
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] - Infinite resolution (lines have no pixel edges in true vector)
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] • VectorMAME and AdvanceMAME* support analog output via serial-to-DAC interfaces,
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] but the display remains the limiting factor.
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Practical Build Today
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] The laser galvo route is the only scalable solution. Projects like ZVG (Zektor
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Vector Generator) or PiDP-1 prove it works: replace the CRT deflection with galvanometers,
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] keep the original game ROMs and vector generator logic, output analog X/Y/Z to the laser
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] controller.
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] Result: A display that draws true mathematical lines, not pixels, with brightness control per
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] segment—Atari's original intent, just with coherent light instead of cathode rays.
2026-02-04 00:48:49 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:53:36 [scudmarks@discord] on ali express i see:
2026-02-04 00:53:36 [scudmarks@discord] > NZ$131.97
2026-02-04 00:53:36 [scudmarks@discord] > -22%
2026-02-04 00:53:36 [scudmarks@discord] > Free shipping
2026-02-04 00:53:36 [scudmarks@discord] > 20W 20Khz Laser Galvo Mirror Lens Simulated Engraving for 450nm 1064nm AR400-700nm
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] That AliExpress listing describes an industrial laser marking galvo scanner, not a
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] display-grade vector scanner. The specs reveal the mismatch:
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] The 20KHz Problem
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] • "20KHz" in galvo listings refers to small-signal bandwidth (tiny deflections).
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] For vector graphics, you care about full-scale slew rate*—how fast the mirrors swing 20–30
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] degrees edge-to-edge.
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - Atari's AVG (Analog Vector Generator) output 15–40K points/second depending on game
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] (Asteroids: ~20K, Tempest: ~40K with double buffer)
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - Industrial 20KHz galvos typically manage only 2–5K points/second at full deflection
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] before distorting into curves
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - The "20KHz" spec assumes 1° optical deflection; at 30° (full screen), bandwidth drops to ~3–5KHz
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] due to inertia
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] • Result*: Your Asteroids ship would draw with visible "s-curves" at the edges instead of
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] crisp lines, or the display would flicker horribly as the galvos fail to settle before the next
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] vector.
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] The "20W" Misdirection
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] That power rating refers to laser handling capacity (likely 1064nm fiber laser for metal
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] marking), not electrical power. For a vector display:
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - You need <100mW visible laser (445nm blue or 532nm green) for indoor projection
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - The 20W rating suggests heavy mirrors designed for industrial YAG lasers—not the lightweight
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] beryllium mirrors needed for high-speed scanning
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - Heavy mirrors = slower acceleration = worse vector fidelity
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] What's Actually in the Box
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] For NZ$131, you're getting:
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - Two closed-loop galvo motors (probably 10mm or 14mm mirror apertures)
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - Driver boards (analog ±10V input, hopefully)
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - No laser source (you supply 450nm/1064nm separately)
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - No safety interlocks (critical failure)
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] The wavelength range (AR400-700nm) means the mirrors are coated for visible light through
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] near-IR—fine for 445nm blue lasers, but the "450nm" spec suggests they're optimizing for cheap
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] Chinese blue diodes, not the 633nm HeNe red of vintage vector games.
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] The Real Requirements
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] For authentic Atari vector rendering, you need:
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] ╭───────────────────────────────┬────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] │  Spec                         │  Industrial 20KHz  │  Display-Grade (Cambridge/Scanlab)  │
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] ├───────────────────────────────┼────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] │  **Slew rate**                │  10,000°/s         │  60,000°/s+                         │
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] │  **Settling time** (to 0.1%)  │  2ms               │  300μs                              │
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] │  **Points/sec** (usable)      │  ~5,000            │  ~50,000+                           │
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] │  **Cost**                     │  $80 USD           │  $800–2,000 USD used                │
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] ╰───────────────────────────────┴────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] • Alternative: Look for "ILDA 30K" or "ILDA 40K"* galvos specifically.
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] These are designed for laser light shows, not engraving, and actually hit the point rates needed
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] for Tempest's dense wireframes.
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] Safety Reality Check
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] If you pair those galvos with a 20W laser (even 20W optical output), you're building a Class 4
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] industrial hazard:
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - Instant retinal damage from diffuse reflections
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - Fire hazard at focal point
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] - Requires interlocked enclosure, beam dumps, and protective eyewear rated for your specific
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] wavelength
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] For a vector display, use <50mW 445nm blue or <20mW 532nm green with proper beam
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] expansion (divergence) to keep irradiance below exposure limits.
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] • Verdict: That $130 part will technically move a laser beam, but it'll draw Asteroids like
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] a drunk Etch-A-Sketch. Fine for experimenting with Lissajous patterns, inadequate for gaming. Save
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] for a used Scanlab RTC4/5 setup or settle for a raster-based "vector look"*
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] (Beamdog/VC4000 emulation) if budget-constrained.
2026-02-04 00:54:13 [kimi-k2.5] 
