2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] That listing describes a DJ/club laser projector with ILDA interface—significantly closer
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] to your needs than the industrial engraver, but with critical caveats.
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] The ILDA Difference
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] • "ILDA" (International Laser Display Association) standard means it likely has a DB-25
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] connector accepting analog X/Y signals* (±5V or ±10V, 0–100kHz bandwidth). This is the same
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] electrical interface that Atari's AVG (Analog Vector Generator) outputs, and what VectorMAME
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] generates via DAC boards.
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] • Translation*: You can wire this directly to a Teensy 4.1 with dual DACs running
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] VectorMAME, or to original arcade hardware if you have it.
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] The Missing Spec
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] The listing doesn't specify scan rate (points per second). Critical distinction:
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] - "ILDA 30K" = 30,000 points/second at 8° optical angle. Minimum for flicker-free
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] Asteroids.
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] - "ILDA 40K" = Comfortable for Tempest/Star Wars dense wireframes.
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] - No rating listed = Likely 15–20K at best, which will flicker badly on complex frames.
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] Cheap DJ lasers often quote "30K" but achieve it only with tiny deflection angles (scanning a small
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] square in the center). At full 30° scan angle (full screen), bandwidth drops to ~10K, causing
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] visible "bloom" and curve distortion at screen edges.
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] The Power Hazard
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] • 1W–5W visible lasers are Class 4 weapons*, not displays:
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] Power    Hazard Level
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] ─────────────────────────────────────────
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] <5mW     Class 3R, eye-safe with aversion
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] <50mW    Class 3B, requires safety glasses
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 1W+      Class 4, burns skin, ignites paper, instant retina damage from diffuse reflections
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] For a home vector monitor, you want <100mW (ideally 50mW @ 445nm blue or 30mW @ 532nm
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] green) with beam expansion to reduce intensity. A 1W unit requires:
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] - Neutral density filters (OD 2.0 or 3.0) to attenuate to safe levels
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] - Interlocked enclosure (opening the case kills power)
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] - Beam dump for static periods (laser must not rest on one point)
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] Running a 1W laser at full power in a room without professional safety measures is inviting
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] permanent eye damage or fires.
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] The "3D Animation" Reality
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] This usually means:
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] - Onboard SD card storing pre-rendered frames (circles, spirals, text)
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] - Not real-time 3D mesh rendering
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] - Slow frame rate (10–20 fps) when displaying complex ILDA frames
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] For arcade emulation, you need real-time vector drawing at 60fps minimum. These cheap units
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] often buffer ILDA frames and replay them at fixed rates, introducing latency and judder unsuitable
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] for interactive gaming.
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] Build Quality
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] At NZ230 (~140 USD), you're getting:
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] - Clone galvos: Likely 10mm mirrors with ~15Kpps actual throughput
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] - Poor tuning: "Wobbly" lines due to servo ringing; no fine-tuning of PID loops
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] - False ILDA: Some units have the DB-25 but only accept digital DMX triggers, not analog XY
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] (verify the pinout)
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] The Czech/Mexico shipping origin suggests dropshipping—the units ship from Chinese
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] factories to regional warehouses. No quality difference from direct AliExpress orders.
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] Verdict
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] • Functional but risky*. If you can confirm:
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 1. True analog ILDA XY input (not just ILDA-style connector carrying DMX)
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 2. Minimum 30Kpps scan rate at 30° optical angle
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 3. Ability to reduce power to <100mW or add appropriate filters
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] Then yes, this will draw vector graphics from a 6800 emulator or original hardware. However, at
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] this price point, a used 20-inch oscilloscope (X-Y mode, <$100) provides safer, more linear
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] vector display—just smaller screen size.
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] For authentic large-scale vector gaming, budget 800+ for used Scanlab/Cambridge galvos, or accept
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] that this 230 unit will produce flickery, distorted "vector-like" output suitable for
2026-02-04 01:02:08 [kimi-k2.5] experimentation but not preservation-quality display.
