2026-02-04 18:47:23 [scudmarks@discord] history of ℘
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] The symbol ℘ (U+2118) denotes the Weierstrass elliptic function, introduced by Karl
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] Weierstrass in his mid-19th-century work on complex analysis (specifically in his 1856 memoirs on
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] Abelian functions and later lectures in Berlin).
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] • Key points:*
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] • Etymology: It is a stylized calligraphic lowercase "p," chosen by Weierstrass to
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] represent his specific elliptic function \wp(z; g2, g3), defined by the series:
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5]     \wp(z) = 1⁄z² + \sum_ω \neq 0 \left( 1⁄(z-ω)² - 1⁄ω² \right)
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5]     over lattice periods ω.
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] • Usage: It became standard notation in elliptic function theory (along with invariants
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] g2, g3 and derivative \wp') to distinguish it from Jacobian elliptic functions (sn, cn,
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] dn).
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] • Unicode: Added to Unicode 1.0 (1991) as "SCRIPT CAPITAL P" despite functioning as a
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] lowercase mathematical symbol—a historical naming quirk carried over from early character sets.
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 18:47:37 [kimi-k2.5] It remains the standard symbol in algebraic geometry and number theory for the Weierstrass
