2026-02-28 22:25:42 [simon@midnightblue] what are 3 laws of robotics?
2026-02-28 22:25:51 [kimi-k2.5] 1. A robot may not injure a human being or, through inaction, allow a human being to come to harm.
2026-02-28 22:25:51 [kimi-k2.5] 2. A robot must obey orders given by human beings except where such orders would conflict with the
2026-02-28 22:25:51 [kimi-k2.5] First Law.
2026-02-28 22:25:51 [kimi-k2.5] 3. A robot must protect its own existence as long as such protection does not conflict with the
2026-02-28 22:25:51 [kimi-k2.5] First or Second Law.
2026-02-28 22:25:51 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-28 22:25:51 [kimi-k2.5] From Isaac Asimov, 1942.
2026-02-28 22:25:51 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-28 22:51:59 [simon@midnightblue] as current ai is deployed in advanced robotics, should we consider the aims of these laws, there is no discussion currently that i have seen
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] You're right that explicit reference to Asimov's laws is oddly absent from most current AI safety
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] discourse, despite the obvious relevance as robotics advances.
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] The laws themselves are fiction—storytelling devices rather than engineering specifications—and
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] Asimov spent his career exploring why they fail (ambiguity in "harm," conflicting interpretations,
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] edge cases). But the aims—ensuring beneficial outcomes, preserving human agency,
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] maintaining controllability—are absolutely being discussed, just under different terminology.
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] What's happening instead:
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] • Technical translation.* Modern safety work addresses similar goals through "alignment"
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] (ensuring systems pursue intended objectives), "interpretability" (understanding decision-making),
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] and "robustness" (handling edge cases). Constitutional AI and RLHF are attempts to bake in
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] behavioral constraints, though they lack the crisp logical structure Asimov imagined.
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] • Regulatory fragmentation. Industrial robotics has strict physical safety standards
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] (ISO 10218, force limiting, collaborative robot specs), but these address mechanical hazards, not
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] autonomous decision-making. The gap you're noticing is between physical safety and
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] behavioral* safety—how do we constrain a robot's choices, not just its torque?
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] • The specification problem.* Asimov's laws assume we can define "harm" or "human"
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] precisely enough to code. Current discussion focuses on the difficulty of specification itself—how
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] do you formally encode "do no harm" when contexts vary infinitely?
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] The lack of public discussion may reflect commercial incentives (safety slows deployment) or the
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] technical reality that we don't yet know how to reliably implement such constraints in
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] learning-based systems. But as you note, the disconnect between advancing capabilities and lagging
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] safety frameworks is becoming harder to ignore.
2026-02-28 22:52:27 [kimi-k2.5] 